pthreads: the fallback signals are cleared before a wait, as meant

When AllocSignal() finds no free signal, pthread_cond_timedwait falls back
to Ctrl-E for the condition and Ctrl-D for its timer, and clears that bit
first so a stale one doesn't end the wait at once. It called
SetSignal(SIGF_..._FALLBACK, 0). exec's SetSignal(newSignals, signalSet)
changes only the bits in signalSet, so a mask of 0 changed nothing: a
Ctrl-E or Ctrl-D already pending (the program's own, or one left from an
earlier fallback wait) woke the wait straight away, as a spurious wakeup,
or a timed wait as if its time had run out.

Now SetSignal(0, SIGF_..._FALLBACK) clears it, as pthread_join's own
fallback (0006) already does with SIGF_PARENT. It matters only when the
task has no free signal bit left.

Test: tests/pthreads/test_fallback_clear.sh.

--- a/pthreads/pthread.c
+++ b/pthreads/pthread.c
@@ -1099,7 +1099,7 @@
 		signal = AllocSignal(-1);
 		if (signal == -1) {
 			signal = SIGB_TIMER_FALLBACK;
-			SetSignal(SIGF_TIMER_FALLBACK, 0);
+			SetSignal(0, SIGF_TIMER_FALLBACK);	// clear it: (new, mask) sets only the bits in mask
 		}
 		timermp.mp_SigBit = signal;
 		NEWLIST(&timermp.mp_MsgList);
@@ -1148,7 +1148,7 @@
 	signal = AllocSignal(-1);
 	if (signal == -1) {
 		signal = SIGB_COND_FALLBACK;
-		SetSignal(SIGF_COND_FALLBACK, 0);
+		SetSignal(0, SIGF_COND_FALLBACK);	// clear it, as the timer's above
 	}
 	waiter.sigmask = 1 << signal;
 	waiter.signalled = FALSE;
